WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.641
This video explains how backends avoid lost or duplicated orders when a database and a message queue must agree.

00:00:07.841 --> 00:00:09.884
Picture a restaurant ticket rail.

00:00:10.284 --> 00:00:17.308
The waiter writes the order and the kitchen ticket in one motion, so one can't exist without the other. That is the outbox.

00:00:17.708 --> 00:00:23.084
A runner carries tickets to the kitchen. If unsure whether a ticket arrived, they carry it again.

00:00:23.484 --> 00:00:28.372
The kitchen ignores any ticket number it already cooked. That is an idempotent consumer.

00:00:28.772 --> 00:00:35.993
And if the delivery can't happen, earlier steps are undone in reverse: refund the card, release the table. That is a saga.

00:00:37.193 --> 00:00:38.470
Let's walk a failure.

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:43.003
The order and its outbox row are written in one database transaction.

00:00:43.403 --> 00:00:46.259
The relay publishes the event with a stable id.

00:00:46.659 --> 00:00:48.621
The acknowledgement gets lost.

00:00:49.021 --> 00:00:53.619
So the relay publishes the same event again. This is at-least-once delivery.

00:00:54.019 --> 00:00:58.988
The consumer has already seen that id, so it skips it. The effect happens once.

00:00:59.388 --> 00:01:01.652
Finally the relay marks the row as sent.

00:01:02.852 --> 00:01:03.374
Recap.

00:01:03.774 --> 00:01:06.944
Never write the database and the queue as two separate steps.

00:01:07.344 --> 00:01:10.502
Outbox plus relay gives at-least-once delivery.

00:01:10.902 --> 00:01:13.119
Consumers dedupe by event id.

00:01:13.519 --> 00:01:16.410
Sagas undo completed steps in reverse order.

00:01:16.810 --> 00:01:20.340
Practice in lesson forty point one and design sd twenty six.
